statement.text:
We call a group G containing an involution i a T0 -group if
    1) the order of the product of any two involutions conjugate to i is finite;
    2) all 2-subgroups of G are either cyclic or generalized quaternion;
    3) the centralizer C of the involution i in G is infinite, distinct from G, and has
finite periodic part;
    4) the normalizer of any non-trivial i-invariant finite subgroup in G either is con-
tained in C or has periodic part which is a Frobenius group (see 6.55) with abelian
kernel and finite complement of even order;
    5) for every element c not contained in C for which ci is an involution there is an
element s of C such that ⟨c, cs ⟩ is an infinite subgroup.
    Does there exist a simple T0 -group?                                   V. P. Shunkov
